    "report": "Messaging and Notifications no longer read the machine's time. Their times now come from the same clock the rest of OpenStay uses, passed in from `createApplication`. The work is committed on `main` as `c56027a`, not pushed.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Messaging domain:** `Conversation.start`, `send` and `report` require their time (`sentAt`, `reportedAt`) and no longer default to `new Date()`.\n- **Messaging wiring:** the `clock` in `messagingWiring` is required, not defaulted. `ReportMessageCommandHandler` now takes the clock, as the other handlers do. `StartConversation` and `SendMessage` no longer default it.\n- **Notifications domain:** `NoticeWindow.admit`, `MessageNoticeOwing.owe` and `Notice.accept` require their time.\n- **Notifications wiring:** `notificationsWiring` takes the clock. `MessageSentIntegrationEventHandler` and `NoticeDelivery` take it and pass its reading to `owe()` and `accept()`.\n- **Specs:** seven spec files that relied on the defaults now pass a fixed test instant, `2026-03-01T10:00:00Z`. No assertions changed.\n\n**Verification**\n- Typecheck: zero errors.\n- Unit tests for Messaging and Notifications: 140 suites, 977 tests, all passing.\n- E2E tests for Messaging and Notifications: 129 suites, 679 tests, all passing.\n- ESLint on the touched directories and `check:conventions`: both pass. Prettier passes on the changed files.\n- A grep of non-test Messaging and Notifications code finds no `new Date()`, `Date.now()` or SQL `now()` left.\n\n**Choices and open points**\n- I made the `sentAt`, `reportedAt` and `at` parameters required rather than removing the defaults and keeping a fallback somewhere else. That matches the rule that a domain method's time is always given.\n- The Notifications clock sits before the message-window parameter in `notificationsWiring`. The window stays defaulted at 900, since it is a length of time, not a moment.\n- The spec files still call `new Date(...)` for their fixed instants. That is test fixture data, not a read of the machine clock, but a strict reading of \"nothing reads the machine's time\" would count it. I left it.\n- I did not start a server, so port 4471 was not used.",
